{"kind":"definition","paper":"2307.02473","version":"1","local":"defn-1","file":"","label":"zircon","number":"2.1","section":"sec-3","order":1,"title":"Definition","body_md":"\\label{zircon}\r\nA poset $P$ is a \\emph{zircon} if, for every non-minimal element $p \\in P$, the principal order ideal $P_{\\leq p}$ is finite and admits a special matching.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"Defn","item":"definition","head":"environment","counter":"Thm","number_from":"counter","cites_in_head":["Hultman_zircon"],"labels":["zircon"],"scope":"paper","origin":"cited","span":{"file":"Automorphisms_arXiv.tex","from":301,"to":303},"hash":"sha256:b7bd8a3fe9efb76ea39ff14ec5c65073215f0fd7e8a28dcc1900ab5025e0bd75","minhash":"","census":"2307.02473v1#env-1","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"definition","paper":"2307.02473","version":"1","local":"defn-2","file":"","label":"spm","number":"2.2","section":"sec-3","order":2,"title":"Definition","body_md":"\\label{spm}\r\nLet $P$ be a finite poset with $\\hat{1}$ and covering relation $\\vartriangleleft$.\r\nA \\emph{special partial matching}, or \\emph{SPM}, on $P$ is a function $M \\colon P \\to P$ such that\r\n\\begin{itemize}\r\n  \\item $M^2=\\mathrm{id}$,\r\n  \\item $M(\\hat{1}) \\vartriangleleft \\hat{1}$,\r\n  \\item for all $x \\in P$, we have $M(x) \\vartriangleleft x$, $x \\vartriangleleft M(x)$, or $M(x)=x$, and\r\n  \\item if $x \\vartriangleleft y$ and $M(x) \\neq y$, then $M(x)\u003cM(y)$.\r\n\\end{itemize}","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"Defn","item":"definition","head":"environment","counter":"Thm","number_from":"counter","cites_in_head":["A-H"],"labels":["spm"],"scope":"paper","origin":"cited","span":{"file":"Automorphisms_arXiv.tex","from":308,"to":317},"hash":"sha256:f1a409659e4236e83bb1adbcf3f08a1edc750764da0fd14058116ab4d2dd0ca9","minhash":"","census":"2307.02473v1#env-2","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2307.02473","version":"1","local":"lemma-1","file":"","label":"lifting","number":"2.3","section":"sec-3","order":3,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{lifting}\r\nSuppose that $P$ is a finite poset with $\\hat{1}$ and an SPM $M$. If $x,y \\in P$ with $x\u003cy$ and $M(y) \\leq y$, then\r\n\\begin{itemize}\r\n  \\item[(i)]   $M(x) \\leq y$,\r\n  \\item[(ii)]  $M(x) \\leq x \\Rightarrow M(x)\u003cM(y)$, and\r\n  \\item[(iii)] $M(x) \\geq x \\Rightarrow x \\leq M(y)$.\r\n \\end{itemize}","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"Lemma","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"Thm","number_from":"counter","name":"Lifting property","cites_in_head":["A-H-H_pircons"],"labels":["lifting"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"cited","origin":"cited","span":{"file":"Automorphisms_arXiv.tex","from":322,"to":329},"hash":"sha256:5ff0acd5719d9e7be6a886ec7fd8ba1147236e7cd6eb4735e16730dc44014082","minhash":"","census":"2307.02473v1#env-3","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"definition","paper":"2307.02473","version":"1","local":"defn-3","file":"","label":"pircon","number":"2.4","section":"sec-3","order":4,"title":"Definition","body_md":"\\label{pircon}\r\nA poset $P$ is a \\emph{pircon} if, for every non-minimal element $p \\in P$, the principal order ideal $P_{\\leq p}$ is finite and admits an SPM.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"Defn","item":"definition","head":"environment","counter":"Thm","number_from":"counter","cites_in_head":["A-H-H_pircons"],"labels":["pircon"],"scope":"paper","origin":"cited","span":{"file":"Automorphisms_arXiv.tex","from":333,"to":335},"hash":"sha256:ee4a041c2036519e2d9f550003f979ac16e1ecaf1ea559ad72b2169122c4b3f5","minhash":"","census":"2307.02473v1#env-4","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"definition","paper":"2307.02473","version":"1","local":"defn-4","file":"","label":"order","number":"2.5","section":"sec-4","order":5,"title":"Definition","body_md":"\\label{order}\r\nLet $\\sigma,\\tau \\in S_n^B$. Then $\\sigma \\leq \\tau$ if and only if $\\sigma[i,j] \\leq \\tau[i,j]$ for all $(i,j) \\in [\\pm n]^2$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"Defn","item":"definition","head":"environment","counter":"Thm","number_from":"counter","labels":["order"],"scope":"paper","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Automorphisms_arXiv.tex","from":375,"to":377},"hash":"sha256:f6c19cc51de2da8ae4a84f72d3426048622f8ac10259f562c78a1c6edadea6a2","minhash":"","census":"2307.02473v1#env-5","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"statement","subkind":"theorem","paper":"2307.02473","version":"1","local":"thm-1","file":"","label":"thm:autom","number":"3.1","section":"sec-5","order":6,"title":"Theorem","body_md":"\\label{thm:autom}\r\nLet $P$ be a finite poset with a maximum and a special partial matching $M$.\r\nLet also $\\theta$ be any automorphism of $P$.\r\nThen the subposet $P^\\theta$ induced by the fixed elements of $\\theta$ has a special partial matching.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"Thm","item":"statement/theorem","head":"environment","counter":"Thm","number_from":"counter","labels":["thm:autom"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Automorphisms_arXiv.tex","from":398,"to":402},"hash":"sha256:879f8e8baaee42cb022dee9433cb37db03fb36e87478be38c1a016e635f2bc3d","minhash":"","census":"2307.02473v1#env-6","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"proof","paper":"2307.02473","version":"1","local":"proof-1","file":"","label":"prim","section":"sec-5","order":7,"title":"Proof","body_md":"Since $P$ is finite, $\\theta$ has finite order $K$.\r\nObserve that each automorphism $\\theta^i$, $i \\in [K]$, transforms the SPM $M$ into an SPM $M_i$, i.e., $\\theta^i \\circ M=M_i \\circ \\theta^i$.\r\nIn particular, $M_K=M$.\r\n\r\nFor a given $p \\in P$, define\r\n\\[\r\nC(p)=\\{a \\in P \\mid \\text{$a=M_{i_t} \\circ M_{i_{t-1}} \\circ \\cdots \\circ M_{i_1}(p)$ for some $i_1,\\ldots,i_t \\in [K]$}\\}.\r\n\\]\r\nThus, $C(p)$ consists of the elements that are connected to $p$ by the SPMs $M_i$.\r\nBy abuse of notation, we also let $C(p)$ denote the subposet of $P$ induced by the set $C(p)$.\r\nFor $a \\in C(p)$ given by $a=M_{i_t} \\circ M_{i_{t-1}} \\circ \\cdots \\circ M_{i_1}(p)$,\r\ndefine $b \\in C(p)$ as $b=M_{i_t}' \\circ M_{i_{t-1}}' \\circ \\cdots \\circ M_{i_1}'(p)$, where the $M_{i_j}'$ are recursively given by\r\n\\begin{equation} \\label{prim}\r\nM_{i_j}'=\\begin{cases}M_{i_j} \u0026 \\text{if $M_{i_j} \\circ M_{i_{j-1}}' \\circ \\cdots \\circ M_{i_1}'(p)\u003cM_{i_{j-1}}' \\circ \\cdots \\circ M_{i_1}'(p)$,} \\\\\r\n\\mathrm{id} \u0026 \\text{otherwise.}\\end{cases}\r\n\\end{equation}\r\n\r\nFor $a_j,b_j \\in C(p)$, where $a_j=M_{i_j} \\circ \\cdots \\circ M_{i_1}(p)$ and $b_j=M_{i_j}' \\circ \\cdots \\circ M_{i_1}'(p)$, $1 \\leq j \\leq t$, we have $b_j \\leq a_j$.\r\nTo see it, we use induction by assuming that $b_{j-1} \\leq b_{a-1}$.\r\nWe have the following three cases.\r\n\\begin{itemize}\r\n  \\item[(i)]   If $a_j \\geq a_{j-1}$, there is nothing to prove.\r\n  \\item[(ii)]  If $a_j\u003ca_{j-1}$ and $M_{i_j}'=M_{i_j}$, we have $b_j= M_{i_j}(b_{j-1})\u003cb_{j-1}$ and $M_{i_j}(a_{j-1})=a_j\u003ca_{j-1}$.\r\nHence, we may apply the lifting property to get $b_j \\leq a_j$ (with equality if and only if $a_{j-1}=b_{j-1}$).\r\n  \\item[(iii)] Finally, if $a_j\u003ca_{j-1}$ and $M_{i_j}'=\\mathrm{id}$, we have $M_{i_j}(b_{j-1}) \\geq b_{j-1}$ and $M_{i_j}(a_{j-1})=a_j\u003ca_{j-1}$.\r\nWe can then apply the lifting property to get $b_j=b_{j-1} \\leq a_j$.\r\n\\end{itemize}\r\n\r\nThus $b_j \\leq a_j$, proving the claim. In particular, $b \\leq a$.\r\nBy construction, $b \\leq p$, so if $p$ and $a$ are both minimal in $C(p)$, we have $a=b=p$.\r\nHence $C(p)$ contains a unique minimal element.\r\nAnalogously, if we reverse the inequality in \\eqref{prim}, $C(p)$ also contains a unique maximal element. \r\n\\footnote{Observe that $P$ need not have a minimum.\r\nHowever, Lemma~\\ref{lifting} will still hold, with the same proof as in \\cite{A-H-H_pircons}, even if $P$ does not have a maximum, as long as $M$ satisfies the other three conditions in Definition~\\ref{spm}.}\r\n\r\nIf $p$ is not minimal in $C(p)$, then $M_i(p) \\vartriangleleft p$ for some $i$.\r\nTo see this, note that if $a\u003cp$ in the above construction, then $b \\neq p$ and so we may choose $i=i_j$ for the minimal $j$ which satisfies $M_{i_j}'=M_{i_j}$.\r\nReversing the inequality in \\eqref{prim}, a similar reasoning shows that if $p$ is not maximal in $C(p)$, then there is at least one $i$ such that $p \\vartriangleleft M_i(p)$.\r\n\r\nIf $p \\in P^\\theta$ and $p \\leq M_i(p)$ for at least one $i$, then this will hold for all $i$ and hence $p=\\min C(p)$.\r\nSimilarly, if $p \\in P^\\theta$ and $M_i(p) \\leq p$, then $p=\\max C(p)$.\r\nTherefore, for any $p \\in P^\\theta$ we have $p=\\min C(p) $ or $p=\\max C(p)$, both if $C(p)=\\{p\\}$.\r\n\r\nSince $\\theta$ permutes the SPMs $M_i$, we have $\\theta(C(p))=C(\\theta(p))$ for all $p \\in P$. Thus the claim below follows.\r\n\r\n\\begin{ClaimA} \\label{claim:c1}\r\nIf $p \\in P^\\theta$, then both $\\min C(p)$ and $\\max C(p)$ belong to $P^\\theta$.\r\n\\end{ClaimA}\r\n\r\nMoreover we have:\r\n\r\n\\begin{ClaimA} \\label{claim:c2}\r\nFor any $x,y \\in P$, if $\\min C(x) \\leq \\max C(y)$, then $\\min C(x) \\leq \\min C(y)$ and $\\max C(x) \\leq \\max C(y)$.\r\n\\end{ClaimA}\r\n\r\n\\begin{proof}\r\nLet $p \\in P$ be arbitrary. If $s=\\max C(s)$ and $C(s) \\neq C(p)$, it follows from the lifting property that $s\u003ep \\Rightarrow s\u003eM_i(p)$ for all $i$.\r\nThis shows that $s\u003ep \\Rightarrow s\u003e\\max C(p)$ (by repeated application of the lifting property).\r\nA similar argument shows that if $s=\\min C(s)$ and $C(s) \\neq C(p)$, then $s\u003cp \\Rightarrow s\u003c\\min C(p)$.\r\nHence, by using the above argument, if we take $s=\\max C(y)$ and $p=\\min C(x)$ where $s\u003ep$, we get $\\max C(y) \\geq \\max C(x)$.\r\nSimilarly, if $s=\\min C(x)$ and $p=\\max C(y)$ where $s\u003cp$, then $\\min C(x) \\leq \\min C(y)$.\r\n\\end{proof}\r\n\r\nLet $\\vartriangleleft_\\theta$ denote the covering relation in $P^\\theta$.\r\n\r\n\\begin{ClaimA} \\label{claim:c3}\r\nIf $\\alpha=\\min C(p)$ and $\\beta=\\max C(p)$ belong to $P^\\theta$, then $\\alpha \\vartriangleleft_\\theta \\beta$ or $\\alpha=\\beta$.\r\n\\end{ClaimA}\r\n\r\n\\begin{proof}\r\nAssume that $x\\in P^\\theta$. If $x=\\min C(x)$ and $x\u003c\\beta$, then by Claim~\\ref{claim:c2}, $x \\leq \\alpha$.\r\nSimilarly, if $x=\\max C(x)$ and $\\alpha\u003cx$, then by Claim~\\ref{claim:c2}, $\\beta \\leq x$.\r\nIn either case, $x$ does not satisfy $\\alpha\u003cx\u003c\\beta$.\r\n\\end{proof}\r\n\r\nDefine the function $\\mathrm{M}^\\theta \\colon P^\\theta\\to P^\\theta$ by\r\n\\[\r\n\\mathrm{M}^\\theta(p)=\\begin{cases}\\max C(p) \u0026 \\text{if $p=\\min C(p)$,} \\\\ \\min C(p) \u0026 \\text{if $p=\\max C(p)$.}\\end{cases}\r\n\\]\r\nBy Claim~\\ref{claim:c1}, $\\mathrm{M}^\\theta$ is well defined.\r\nWe show that $\\mathrm{M}^\\theta$ is an SPM on $P^\\theta$ by verifying the conditions in Definition~\\ref{spm}.\r\n\r\n\\begin{itemize}\r\n  \\item For every $x \\in P^\\theta$, $(\\mathrm{M}^\\theta)^2(x)=x$. That is, $(\\mathrm{M}^\\theta)^2=\\mathrm{id}$.\r\n\r\n  \\item Since $\\theta$ is an automorphism of $P$, $\\theta(\\hat{1})=\\hat{1}$, so $\\hat{1} \\in P^\\theta$.\r\nBecause $M_i$ is an SPM, $M_i(\\hat{1}) \\neq \\hat{1}$, so $\\hat{1} \\neq \\min C(\\hat{1})$.\r\nBy Claim~\\ref{claim:c3}, $\\mathrm{M}^\\theta(\\hat{1})=\\min C(\\hat{1}) \\vartriangleleft_\\theta \\hat{1}$.\r\n\r\n  \\item For any $x \\in P^\\theta$, Claim~\\ref{claim:c3} shows that $\\mathrm{M}^\\theta(x) \\vartriangleleft_\\theta x$, $x \\vartriangleleft_\\theta \\mathrm{M}^\\theta(x)$, or $\\mathrm{M}^\\theta(x)=x$.\r\n\r\n  \\item Let $x \\vartriangleleft_\\theta y$ and $\\mathrm{M}^\\theta(x) \\neq y$. We must show that $\\mathrm{M}^\\theta(x)\u003c\\mathrm{M}^\\theta(y)$.\r\nBy Claim~\\ref{claim:c2}, $\\min C(x) \\leq \\min C(y)$ and $\\max C(x) \\leq \\max C(y)$.\r\nMoreover, both inequalities are strict since $\\mathrm{M}^\\theta(x) \\neq y$.\r\n\\begin{itemize}\r\n  \\item[(i)]   If $x \\neq \\min C(x)$, then $\\mathrm{M}^\\theta(x)=\\min C(x)\u003c\\min C(y) \\leq \\mathrm{M}^\\theta(y)$.\r\n  \\item[(ii)]  If $y \\neq \\max C(y)$, we have $\\mathrm{M}^\\theta(x) \\leq \\max C(x)\u003c\\max C(y)=\\mathrm{M}^\\theta(y)$.\r\n  \\item[(iii)] If $x=\\min C(x)$ and $y=\\max C(y)$, we have $x=\\min C(x)\u003c\\min C(y) \\leq y$ and $x \\leq \\max C(x)\u003c\\max C(y)=y$.\r\nBecause $x \\vartriangleleft_\\theta y$, we conclude that $\\min C(x)=\\max C(x)$ and $\\min C(y)=\\max C(y)$.\r\nHence $\\mathrm{M}^\\theta(x)=x \\vartriangleleft_\\theta y=\\mathrm{M}^\\theta(y)$. \\qedhere\r\n\\end{itemize}\r\n\\end{itemize}","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","labels":["prim","claim:c1","claim:c2","claim:c3"],"scope":"paper","proves":"thm-1","attach":3,"span":{"file":"Automorphisms_arXiv.tex","from":404,"to":507},"hash":"sha256:efd6145ed6e7cffdc435f4edf0735a6902229d1debf4cb504b4d4580e0560d84","minhash":"","census":"2307.02473v1#proof-1","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"statement","subkind":"claim","paper":"2307.02473","version":"1","local":"claima-1","file":"","label":"claim:c1","number":"1","section":"sec-5","order":8,"title":"Claim","body_md":"\\label{claim:c1}\r\nIf $p \\in P^\\theta$, then both $\\min C(p)$ and $\\max C(p)$ belong to $P^\\theta$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"ClaimA","item":"statement/claim","head":"environment","counter":"ClaimA","number_from":"counter","labels":["claim:c1"],"parent":"proof-1","scope":"local","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Automorphisms_arXiv.tex","from":450,"to":452},"hash":"sha256:c22f72ce131afbe8a74f377f4645e3568da13345790942cac150f4efe23adf42","minhash":"","census":"2307.02473v1#env-7","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"statement","subkind":"claim","paper":"2307.02473","version":"1","local":"claima-2","file":"","label":"claim:c2","number":"2","section":"sec-5","order":9,"title":"Claim","body_md":"\\label{claim:c2}\r\nFor any $x,y \\in P$, if $\\min C(x) \\leq \\max C(y)$, then $\\min C(x) \\leq \\min C(y)$ and $\\max C(x) \\leq \\max C(y)$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"ClaimA","item":"statement/claim","head":"environment","counter":"ClaimA","number_from":"counter","labels":["claim:c2"],"parent":"proof-1","scope":"local","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Automorphisms_arXiv.tex","from":456,"to":458},"hash":"sha256:c7c927a2fd24b3fbfe6713ef0ba778b0613834cb5d174b467b592fb8aa5257e0","minhash":"","census":"2307.02473v1#env-8","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"proof","paper":"2307.02473","version":"1","local":"proof-2","file":"","section":"sec-5","order":10,"title":"Proof","body_md":"Let $p \\in P$ be arbitrary. If $s=\\max C(s)$ and $C(s) \\neq C(p)$, it follows from the lifting property that $s\u003ep \\Rightarrow s\u003eM_i(p)$ for all $i$.\r\nThis shows that $s\u003ep \\Rightarrow s\u003e\\max C(p)$ (by repeated application of the lifting property).\r\nA similar argument shows that if $s=\\min C(s)$ and $C(s) \\neq C(p)$, then $s\u003cp \\Rightarrow s\u003c\\min C(p)$.\r\nHence, by using the above argument, if we take $s=\\max C(y)$ and $p=\\min C(x)$ where $s\u003ep$, we get $\\max C(y) \\geq \\max C(x)$.\r\nSimilarly, if $s=\\min C(x)$ and $p=\\max C(y)$ where $s\u003cp$, then $\\min C(x) \\leq \\min C(y)$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","parent":"proof-1","scope":"local","proves":"claima-2","attach":3,"span":{"file":"Automorphisms_arXiv.tex","from":460,"to":466},"hash":"sha256:8ceadcb17387cf3f5ef2ee25b06444e710c6fd925a4951135f2d52a613ad1115","minhash":"","census":"2307.02473v1#proof-2","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"statement","subkind":"claim","paper":"2307.02473","version":"1","local":"claima-3","file":"","label":"claim:c3","number":"3","section":"sec-5","order":11,"title":"Claim","body_md":"\\label{claim:c3}\r\nIf $\\alpha=\\min C(p)$ and $\\beta=\\max C(p)$ belong to $P^\\theta$, then $\\alpha \\vartriangleleft_\\theta \\beta$ or $\\alpha=\\beta$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"ClaimA","item":"statement/claim","head":"environment","counter":"ClaimA","number_from":"counter","labels":["claim:c3"],"parent":"proof-1","scope":"local","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Automorphisms_arXiv.tex","from":470,"to":472},"hash":"sha256:68120704e7ed4aa8e05f695c589ab8d63c6ee1ed959dbfe1c6fe38fddb4357ff","minhash":"","census":"2307.02473v1#env-9","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"proof","paper":"2307.02473","version":"1","local":"proof-3","file":"","section":"sec-5","order":12,"title":"Proof","body_md":"Assume that $x\\in P^\\theta$. If $x=\\min C(x)$ and $x\u003c\\beta$, then by Claim~\\ref{claim:c2}, $x \\leq \\alpha$.\r\nSimilarly, if $x=\\max C(x)$ and $\\alpha\u003cx$, then by Claim~\\ref{claim:c2}, $\\beta \\leq x$.\r\nIn either case, $x$ does not satisfy $\\alpha\u003cx\u003c\\beta$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","parent":"proof-1","scope":"local","proves":"claima-3","attach":3,"span":{"file":"Automorphisms_arXiv.tex","from":474,"to":478},"hash":"sha256:892b9903800cb80fc597afb5c80d3821b33218bffeb8710be1a22e93d37a7de2","minhash":"","census":"2307.02473v1#proof-3","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"statement","subkind":"corollary","paper":"2307.02473","version":"1","local":"cor-1","file":"","label":"cor:autom","number":"3.2","section":"sec-5","order":13,"title":"Corollary","body_md":"\\label{cor:autom}\r\nLet $P$ be a pircon. If $\\theta$ is any automorphism of $P$, then the subposet $P^\\theta$ is a pircon.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"Cor","item":"statement/corollary","head":"environment","counter":"Thm","number_from":"counter","labels":["cor:autom"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Automorphisms_arXiv.tex","from":511,"to":513},"hash":"sha256:1f94269a051f25bd980de6a7d17e6056d32b504281a1ed58ef983f14853ef12a","minhash":"","census":"2307.02473v1#env-10","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"proof","paper":"2307.02473","version":"1","local":"proof-4","file":"","section":"sec-5","order":14,"title":"Proof","body_md":"For any non-minimal element $p \\in P$, the order ideal $P_{\\leq p}$ is finite and has an SPM.\r\nLet $p \\in P^\\theta$ be non-minimal.\r\nThe elements in $P^\\theta_{\\leq p}$ are the fixed elements of the restriction of $\\theta$ to $P_{\\leq p}$.\r\nTherefore, by Theorem~\\ref{thm:autom}, \\smash{$P^\\theta_{\\leq p}$} has an SPM.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"cor-1","attach":3,"span":{"file":"Automorphisms_arXiv.tex","from":515,"to":520},"hash":"sha256:7bbeea77483b27ad32cede488b63d6cf0e3151db2bd8638c16fb31ec740a8fe9","minhash":"","census":"2307.02473v1#proof-4","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"statement","subkind":"corollary","paper":"2307.02473","version":"1","local":"cor-2","file":"","label":"cor:ball","number":"4.1","section":"sec-6","order":15,"title":"Corollary","body_md":"\\label{cor:ball}\r\nThe order complex of (the proper part of) the fixed point free signed involutions is a PL ball.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"Cor","item":"statement/corollary","head":"environment","counter":"Thm","number_from":"counter","labels":["cor:ball"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"none","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Automorphisms_arXiv.tex","from":552,"to":554},"hash":"sha256:c2e3e737cfe9f5eb129dd1587b7417a8a28c4b1137de17a6de6560b995dbde16","minhash":"","census":"2307.02473v1#env-11","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2307.02473","version":"1","local":"lemma-2","file":"","label":"cover","number":"5.1","section":"sec-7","order":16,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{cover}\r\nLet $\\sigma,\\tau \\in I_n$. Then $\\sigma \\vartriangleleft \\tau$ in $I_n$ if and only if $\\tau=\\operatorname{ct}_{(i,j)}(\\sigma)$ for some (necessarily unique) pair $(i,j)$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"Lemma","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"Thm","number_from":"counter","name":"\\mbox{}","cites_in_head":["Incitti_A|\\Th~5.1"],"labels":["cover"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"cited","origin":"cited","span":{"file":"Automorphisms_arXiv.tex","from":676,"to":678},"hash":"sha256:134fea35f1eea564300a31ed59251547b13a60fc43ca58443c6b17881f1d2262","minhash":"","census":"2307.02473v1#env-12","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2307.02473","version":"1","local":"lemma-3","file":"","label":"Bcover","number":"5.2","section":"sec-7","order":17,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{Bcover}\r\nLet $\\sigma,\\tau \\in I_n^B$. Then $\\sigma \\vartriangleleft \\tau$ in $I_n^B$ if and only if $\\tau=\\operatorname{Bct}_{(i,j)}(\\sigma)$ for some (necessarily unique) pair $(i,j)$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"Lemma","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"Thm","number_from":"counter","cites_in_head":["Incitti_B"],"labels":["Bcover"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"cited","origin":"cited","span":{"file":"Automorphisms_arXiv.tex","from":691,"to":693},"hash":"sha256:0519b63dc234ff7070cf9aa52aca392b2f84d7e75f1cc49f991c2fc9f40e203d","minhash":"","census":"2307.02473v1#env-13","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2307.02473","version":"1","local":"lemma-4","file":"","label":"incr","number":"5.3","section":"sec-7","order":18,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{incr}\r\nLet $\\sigma\u003c\\tau$ in $I_n^B$. Then there is exactly one increasing $\\sigma$-$\\tau$-chain, and it is lex-minimal.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"Lemma","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"Thm","number_from":"counter","name":"\\mbox{}","cites_in_head":["Incitti_B|\\Th~4.4"],"labels":["incr"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"cited","origin":"cited","span":{"file":"Automorphisms_arXiv.tex","from":701,"to":703},"hash":"sha256:51e11cfcab412cdb09c2d34e4b3a37efecc74fc2c19c87de213073f11700727d","minhash":"","census":"2307.02473v1#env-14","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"statement","subkind":"lemma","paper":"2307.02473","version":"1","local":"lemma-5","file":"","label":"decr","number":"5.4","section":"sec-7","order":19,"title":"Lemma","body_md":"\\label{decr}\r\nLet $\\sigma\u003c\\tau$ in $I_n^B$. Then there is exactly one decreasing $\\sigma$-$\\tau$-chain.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"Lemma","item":"statement/lemma","head":"environment","counter":"Thm","number_from":"counter","name":"\\mbox{}","cites_in_head":["Incitti_B|\\Th~4.6"],"labels":["decr"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"cited","origin":"cited","span":{"file":"Automorphisms_arXiv.tex","from":705,"to":707},"hash":"sha256:ca65b7cc84e9c86d0d817c7c29fc6b2ed33dc0cec72883e6bbd7b18f2a06fe88","minhash":"","census":"2307.02473v1#env-15","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"remark","paper":"2307.02473","version":"1","local":"remark-1","file":"","section":"sec-7","order":20,"title":"Remark","body_md":"Since $\\operatorname{Bct}_{(i,j)}(\\sigma)(i)\u003e\\operatorname{Bct}_{(i,j)}(\\sigma)(j)$, there is also exactly one $\\sigma$-$\\tau$-chain with weakly decreasing labels.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"Remark","item":"remark","head":"environment","scope":"paper","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Automorphisms_arXiv.tex","from":709,"to":711},"hash":"sha256:1cda9fd011f6cd69dfea67f6ccf1aa91936f5975544c0e98e9364e221b9fe796","minhash":"","census":"2307.02473v1#env-16","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"statement","subkind":"theorem","paper":"2307.02473","version":"1","local":"thm-2","file":"","label":"thm:EL","number":"5.5","section":"sec-7","order":21,"title":"Theorem","body_md":"\\label{thm:EL}\r\nThe poset $F_n^B$ is EL-shellable.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"Thm","item":"statement/theorem","head":"environment","counter":"Thm","number_from":"counter","labels":["thm:EL"],"scope":"paper","proof_status":"here","origin":"own","span":{"file":"Automorphisms_arXiv.tex","from":736,"to":738},"hash":"sha256:a9cc0875808de64fc9260f1d0192be5c00940d101cec1749372a27269868fb5c","minhash":"","census":"2307.02473v1#env-17","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
{"kind":"proof","paper":"2307.02473","version":"1","local":"proof-5","file":"","section":"sec-7","order":22,"title":"Proof","body_md":"Let $\\sigma\u003c\\tau$ in $F_n^B$, let $C_D=(\\sigma \\vartriangleleft \\tau_k \\vartriangleleft \\cdots \\vartriangleleft \\tau_1 \\vartriangleleft \\tau)$ be the decreasing $\\sigma$-$\\tau$-chain in $I_n^B$, where $k \\geq 1$,\r\nand let $(i_\\tau,j_\\tau)$ be the label on $\\tau_1 \\vartriangleleft \\tau$.\r\nObserve that if we can prove that $\\tau_1 \\in F_n^B$, it follows that $\\tau_1,\\ldots,\\tau_k \\in F_n^B$, because the decreasing $\\sigma$-$\\tau_1$-chain in $I_n^B$ is $\\sigma \\vartriangleleft \\tau_k \\vartriangleleft \\cdots \\vartriangleleft \\tau_2 \\vartriangleleft \\tau_1$.\r\n\r\nLet $h=\\operatorname{di}(\\sigma,\\tau)$.\r\nSince $\\sigma$ has no fixed points, $h$ is an exceedance of $\\sigma$ (i.e., $\\sigma(h)\u003eh$).\r\nIndeed, if $\\sigma(i)\u003ci$, then $\\sigma(i)$ is an exceedance of $\\sigma$, and $\\sigma(\\sigma(i))=\\tau(\\sigma(i)) \\Leftrightarrow \\sigma(i)=\\tau(i)$.\r\nConsider any cover $\\pi \\vartriangleleft \\pi'$ in $[\\sigma,\\tau]$.\r\nTwo applications of Definition~\\ref{order} yield $\\operatorname{di}(\\pi,\\pi') \\geq \\operatorname{di}(\\pi,\\tau) \\geq h$.\r\nSince the label $(i,j)$ on $\\pi \\vartriangleleft \\pi'$ satisfies $i=\\operatorname{di}(\\pi,\\pi')$, it follows that $h$ is in some label on $C_D$, and because $C_D$ is decreasing, $i_\\tau=h$.\r\n\r\nSuppose $\\tau_1 \\notin F_n^B$ so that $\\tau_1 \\vartriangleleft \\tau$ is a case where the number of fixed points decreases.\r\nThe only such cases are E1.1, N1.1, N4.2, and H2.1, and in all of them, $h$ is a fixed point of $\\tau_1$.\r\nObserve that $\\tau_1$ has fixed points also in E2.6a, N1.2a, N1.2b, N1.3a, N1.3b, N2.2, H1.2, H2.2a, H2.2b, and H2.3, and $h$ is not always one of them, but the number of fixed points does not decrease in those cases.\r\nTherefore, $\\sigma[h,h+1]\u003e\\tau_1[h,h+1]$.\r\nSince $\\sigma \\leq \\tau_1$, it follows from Definition~\\ref{order} that $\\tau_1 \\in F_n^B$.\r\n\r\nWe have proved that the decreasing $\\sigma$-$\\tau$-chain in $I_n^B$ is contained in $F_n^B$.\r\nThe next step is to show that this chain is lex-maximal.\r\nThis is done in the same way as in the proof of \\cite[Theorem~1.4]{Hansson_graded_A}, where Incitti's type~$A$ version of Lemma~\\ref{incr} is used.\r\nWe include the argument here in order to make the proof complete.\r\n\r\nTo obtain a contradiction, consider the lex-maximal $\\sigma$-$\\tau$-chain in $I_n^B$, and suppose it is not decreasing;\r\ncall it $C=(\\pi_1 \\vartriangleleft \\cdots \\vartriangleleft \\pi_{k+2})$ and say that $\\lambda(\\pi_1,\\pi_2) \\leq \\lambda(\\pi_2,\\pi_3)$.\r\nBy Lemma~\\ref{incr}, $\\pi_1 \\vartriangleleft \\pi_2 \\vartriangleleft \\pi_3$ is lex-minimal among the $\\pi_1$-$\\pi_3$-chains in $I_n^B$.\r\nThus, $\\pi_1 \\vartriangleleft \\pi_2' \\vartriangleleft \\pi_3 \\vartriangleleft \\cdots \\vartriangleleft \\pi_{k+2}$, where $\\pi_1 \\vartriangleleft \\pi_2' \\vartriangleleft \\pi_3$ is the decreasing $\\pi_1$-$\\pi_3$-chain, is lex-larger than $C$, a contradiction.\r\n\r\nSo, the decreasing $\\sigma$-$\\tau$-chain in $I_n^B$ is contained in $F_n^B$ and it is lex-maximal.\r\nNow, by reversing the lexicographic order on the set $\\{(i,j) \\in [\\pm n]^2 \\mid i\u003cj\\}$, we obtain an edge-labelling of $F_n^B$ such that in each interval $[\\sigma,\\tau]$, there is an increasing $\\sigma$-$\\tau$-chain which is lex-minimal.\r\nBy Lemma~\\ref{decr} and the remark following it, this is an EL-labelling of $F_n^B$.","path":"source","confidence":"certain","env":"proof","item":"proof","head":"environment","scope":"paper","proves":"thm-2","attach":3,"span":{"file":"Automorphisms_arXiv.tex","from":740,"to":771},"hash":"sha256:c369c3660d8b4ca5efc22c4226099900d08d2223421464e93ac181aea27f254a","minhash":"","census":"2307.02473v1#proof-5","extractor":"ax/v0.5.8"}
